WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.470 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.446 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.446 --> 00:00:05.928 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.928 --> 00:00:11.698 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.698 --> 00:00:13.990 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Here, I'm
going to try to answer that.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.240 align:middle line:90%
That's a really good question.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.400 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.650 align:middle line:84%
And in some cases, we
just kind have to guess.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:25.633 align:middle line:90%
But in some cases, we have--

00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:27.550 align:middle line:84%
especially for languages
like Greek and Latin,

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.520 align:middle line:84%
we have descriptions
by people who attempted

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.200 align:middle line:90%
to describe their own sounds.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.210 align:middle line:84%
That's a kind of
thing that happens.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:38.140 align:middle line:84%
We can infer things
from mistakes

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.180 align:middle line:84%
that people made
at various points.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.360 align:middle line:84%
So people who were learning
how to spell, or say children,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.510 align:middle line:84%
or people who weren't
highly educated,

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:54.520 align:middle line:84%
when they misspelled words
in, say, Greek or Latin,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.700 align:middle line:90%
you can infer things.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.830 align:middle line:90%
So if you're making--

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:59.000 align:middle line:84%
if a certain kind of
misspelling is common,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.030 align:middle line:84%
you get to infer that those
sounds were kind of common.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.462 align:middle line:84%
So in the case of ancient Greek,
we think that there was an "Y"

00:01:05.462 --> 00:01:07.420 align:middle line:84%
[high front rounded vowel]
partly because there

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.690 align:middle line:84%
was a period where people were--
when people misspelled it.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.080 align:middle line:84%
They either misspelled
it as "u" or as "i".

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.020 align:middle line:84%
And then later, there was a
period where people reliably

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.720 align:middle line:84%
misspelled it as "i,"
leading us to think

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.240 align:middle line:84%
that it had shifted to what
it is in modern Greek, which

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.220 align:middle line:90%
is an "i."

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.740 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the kinds
of things people do.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.380 align:middle line:84%
You also get to look at
what happens to these words

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.170 align:middle line:84%
when they get borrowed
into other languages,

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.480 align:middle line:84%
so languages that have
richer or poorer systems.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.870 align:middle line:84%
There's one classic example
from Greek and from Latin.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.758 align:middle line:90%
We know that Greek--

00:01:44.758 --> 00:01:46.570 align:middle line:84%
I see, I'm sorry,
Raquel, you were right.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.362 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be a
long-- a long answer.

00:01:48.362 --> 00:01:51.940 align:middle line:84%
We know that the Greek
letter, this Greek letter,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.510 align:middle line:84%
used to be pronounced as a
"p" with an extra puff of air.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.260 align:middle line:84%
It used to be pi or pea
[pronounced with aspiration],

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:02.020 align:middle line:84%
so just an especially
emphatic "p."

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.930 align:middle line:84%
They also had
another letter, which

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.960 align:middle line:84%
was a "p" without the
extra puff of air, "p."

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.780 align:middle line:90%
And then this one shifted to--

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.390 align:middle line:84%
well, actually, the modern--
this is the symbol for it

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:18.970 align:middle line:84%
in IPA, the bilabial
fricative, a "fuh," a "fuh."

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.190 align:middle line:84%
And the way that we
know-- we can actually

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.650 align:middle line:84%
date pretty precisely
when this shift happened.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.570 align:middle line:84%
And that's because Greek
was loaning lots of words

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:29.300 align:middle line:90%
into Latin.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.180 align:middle line:84%
Latin was borrowing
words from Greek,

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.465 align:middle line:84%
including a word that
they spelled like this.

00:02:34.465 --> 00:02:38.550 align:middle line:90%


00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:42.800 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that they spelled
it like that, first of all,

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.872 align:middle line:84%
suggests that it
was a "p" with an--

00:02:44.872 --> 00:02:46.580 align:middle line:84%
a "p" with an "h"
sound and not something

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.940 align:middle line:84%
more like an "f" because
Latin had an "F."

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.550 align:middle line:84%
And they would have
used an F if they had

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:53.930 align:middle line:90%
thought that this was an "f."

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.260 align:middle line:84%
Also, because people who were
bad at spelling-- children,

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.980 align:middle line:84%
and people writing graffiti,
and stuff like that--

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.740 align:middle line:84%
when they misspelled this word,
when they were writing graffiti

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.620 align:middle line:84%
about philosophy,
they would misspell it

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.070 align:middle line:84%
by leaving out the H. So they
would spell it just with a P.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:11.810 align:middle line:84%
And then later, there
was a period where

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.352 align:middle line:84%
people who were
misspelling this word

00:03:13.352 --> 00:03:15.800 align:middle line:84%
would misspell it by
writing it with an F.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.770 align:middle line:84%
And that's how you know
that the word had changed

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.410 align:middle line:84%
its pronunciation right
then at that point

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:26.570 align:middle line:84%
from an aspirated "p," a "p"
with an "h" sound, to an "f."

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:28.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So when
I misspell things,

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.660 align:middle line:84%
that's actually
benefiting the future.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.952 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You're being--

00:03:30.952 --> 00:03:34.610 align:middle line:84%
providing valuable data for
future historical linguists,

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.450 align:middle line:90%
absolutely.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.450 align:middle line:90%
That's absolutely true.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, please engrave your
misspellings in stone.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.690 align:middle line:84%
That will make them a lot
easier for people to handle.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.996 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions about this?

00:03:43.996 --> 00:03:48.170 align:middle line:84%
OK, where was I
going with all that?

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.870 align:middle line:90%
Oh, right, so I was saying--

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.120 align:middle line:84%
I was telling you some
things about typology.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.950 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
have poorer and richer vowel

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:56.450 align:middle line:90%
systems.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.940 align:middle line:84%
English has a particularly
rich system of vowels.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.895 align:middle line:84%
We have a fairly large
number of vowels.

00:04:00.895 --> 00:04:03.020 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty of languages
out there that have--

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.500 align:middle line:84%
languages that have
fewer, that have five.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.990 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty of
languages that have three.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:12.140 align:middle line:84%
If you have three
vowels, your three vowels

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:17.000 align:middle line:84%
are pretty invariably
"a," "i," and "u"

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.040 align:middle line:84%
That is, they're the
vowels that are kind of

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.290 align:middle line:90%
in the corners of this system.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.230 align:middle line:84%
They're at the bottom
right, and the top right,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.958 align:middle line:90%
and the top left, yeah.

00:04:25.958 --> 00:04:28.250 align:middle line:84%
There aren't languages out
there that have three vowels

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:31.520 align:middle line:84%
and the three vowels are
"eh," "ih," and "ah."

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.000 align:middle line:84%
That's not a kind of
language that we find.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.790 align:middle line:84%
And there's some work
on this, including

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.540 align:middle line:84%
classic work by my
colleague Edward Fleming,

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.270 align:middle line:84%
talking about
optimal distributions

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:46.970 align:middle line:84%
of vowels and vowel
space, where the idea is

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:51.200 align:middle line:90%
have this space in your mouth.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.860 align:middle line:84%
You want your vowels to
be maximally contrastive.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.320 align:middle line:84%
So you want people to always
be able to hear the difference

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:58.520 align:middle line:90%
between different vowels.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.603 align:middle line:84%
And the best way to do
that is to have your vowels

00:05:00.603 --> 00:05:04.280 align:middle line:84%
as far apart from each other as
possible, as a theory about why

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.860 align:middle line:84%
we get the kinds of vowel
systems that we get and not

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.390 align:middle line:90%
others.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.113 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:05:12.113 --> 00:05:13.780 align:middle line:84%
Oh, right, and then
there are languages,

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.513 align:middle line:84%
like French, that
have nasalized vowels.

00:05:16.513 --> 00:05:19.180 align:middle line:84%
So French has words like "main,"
[with nasalized vowel] which is

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.790 align:middle line:90%
hand, and "met," which is dish.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.490 align:middle line:84%
So that's another option
that some languages take.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.290 align:middle line:84%
You can lower your
velum and allow some air

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:29.770 align:middle line:84%
to flow through your nasal
cavity as you're making sounds.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.110 align:middle line:84%
French, Portuguese, lots
of languages do this.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.060 align:middle line:90%


00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.150 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I said we're
classifying vowels.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.440 align:middle line:90%
Why are we classifying vowels?

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:40.880 align:middle line:84%
Partly because
it's fun, but also

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.810 align:middle line:84%
because it enables us to
realize that we wonder

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.450 align:middle line:90%
about kinds of vowel spaces.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.420 align:middle line:84%
Why are all the English vowels
in the upper right-hand corner?

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:51.565 align:middle line:90%
Do they have to be?

00:05:51.565 --> 00:05:53.690 align:middle line:84%
If you had a rounded vowel
that was somewhere else,

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:54.732 align:middle line:90%
what would it sound like?

00:05:54.732 --> 00:05:56.310 align:middle line:90%
All that good stuff.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.340 align:middle line:84%
Another thing that
it does is help

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:01.700 align:middle line:84%
us to develop theories of
phonologically natural sound

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:02.370 align:middle line:90%
changes.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.760 align:middle line:90%
So here are some Turkish nouns.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, I'm going to
use your native language.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.070 align:middle line:84%
And I'll just ask
you to be quiet

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.450 align:middle line:90%
for the next little while.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.670 align:middle line:90%
Here are some Turkish nouns.

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:16.160 align:middle line:84%
Some of you, if you've
ever read CS Lewis,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.180 align:middle line:84%
you might recognize
the first one.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:25.070 align:middle line:84%
And here are the
plurals of these nouns.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.140 align:middle line:84%
What's the plural
suffix in Turkish?

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.320 align:middle line:90%


00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:31.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:32.850 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: L-A-R.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.810 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
it's L-A-R, [TURKISH]

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:40.073 align:middle line:84%
like in the word for lions,
and the word for arms,

00:06:40.073 --> 00:06:42.240 align:middle line:84%
and the word for slaves,
and the word for daughters.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.710 align:middle line:84%
Oh, but not in the words
for winds, teeth, and roses,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.580 align:middle line:84%
where we seem to
get a [TURKISH]..

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.500 align:middle line:90%
So we get L-E-R for those three.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:56.760 align:middle line:90%
I'll write them down--

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:57.510 align:middle line:90%
[TURKISH]

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:01.770 align:middle line:90%


00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.150 align:middle line:90%
And we get [TURKISH] for these--

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:06.870 align:middle line:90%
[TURKISH]

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:17.340 align:middle line:90%


00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.470 align:middle line:84%
Having done that, I'm now going
to go back to the last slide.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.260 align:middle line:90%


00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:25.060 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.610 align:middle line:84%
Why are we getting [TURKISH]
with these and [TURKISH]

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:28.180 align:middle line:90%
with those?

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.040 align:middle line:90%


00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.740 align:middle line:84%
Joseph, what do you
think is happening?

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think this is
an example of vowel harmony.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.198 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think
this is an example of what's

00:07:37.198 --> 00:07:38.450 align:middle line:90%
called vowel harmony.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.550 align:middle line:90%
What do you mean?

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:42.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So a lot
of languages will

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.920 align:middle line:84%
have a certain suffix
or a prefix, affix

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:47.310 align:middle line:90%
without a morpheme.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.780 align:middle line:84%
And when you attach to
another word, the vowel,

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:55.400 align:middle line:84%
the primary vowel in that
found morpheme will change to--

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.860 align:middle line:90%
will harmonize with whatever--

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:02.770 align:middle line:84%
with some vowel in the other
[? word ?] [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.320 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
that's well said.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:12.900 align:middle line:84%
So what does this vowel have
in common with these vowels?

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.860 align:middle line:84%
And what does this vowel have
in common with these vowels?

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:26.700 align:middle line:84%
Bear in mind that "oo" is
a front high grounded vowel

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:35.610 align:middle line:84%
and "uh" is a back
high unrounded vowel.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:40.530 align:middle line:84%
What do these vowels, "ay,"
"ee," and "oo" all have

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:41.500 align:middle line:90%
in common?

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:42.120 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:43.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: They're front.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.650 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
They're all front.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:46.790 align:middle line:90%
And what do these vowels--

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:50.090 align:middle line:90%
"ah," "oh," "oo," and "uh"--

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:50.910 align:middle line:90%
have in common?

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:54.350 align:middle line:90%
Well, they're all back, right?

00:08:54.350 --> 00:09:00.650 align:middle line:84%
And the vowel in "lar" is
back and the vowel in "ler"

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:02.240 align:middle line:90%
is front.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.450 align:middle line:84%
So just as Joseph said,
this is vowel harmony.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.820 align:middle line:84%
The vowel of this
suffix is having

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:12.475 align:middle line:84%
its properties determined by
the vowel of the thing it's

00:09:12.475 --> 00:09:14.600 align:middle line:84%
being attached to, the noun
it's being attached to.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:18.170 align:middle line:84%
Whether it's front
or back is determined

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.820 align:middle line:84%
by the vowel of the
noun it's attached to.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.880 align:middle line:90%
So this is front-back harmony.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.550 align:middle line:90%
There's also rounding harmony--

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:28.640 align:middle line:84%
so cases where you have a
suffix whose roundedness

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.070 align:middle line:84%
is determined by the
roundedness of the vowel

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:32.305 align:middle line:90%
that it's attaching to.

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:33.680 align:middle line:84%
There are other
kinds of harmony.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.270 align:middle line:84%
But those are
things that happen.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:38.180 align:middle line:84%
And categorizing vowels,
looking at vowels and thinking,

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.520 align:middle line:84%
ah yes, some of these are
front and others are back,

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.557 align:middle line:84%
it's not just a fun
thing to do that allows

00:09:42.557 --> 00:09:44.390 align:middle line:84%
us to think about the
insides of our mouths.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:47.018 align:middle line:84%
It allows us to understand
Turkish better, right?

00:09:47.018 --> 00:09:48.560 align:middle line:84%
What the heck is
going on in Turkish?

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.080 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's harmony
for front versus back.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:56.440 align:middle line:90%


00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:02.230 align:middle line:84%
OK, I think when I was first
introducing you to consonants,

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:09.370 align:middle line:84%
I said, consonants, they involve
disturbing the flow of air.

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:14.660 align:middle line:84%
And the flow of air is usually
going outward from your lungs.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.150 align:middle line:84%
But there are some
other options,

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.910 align:middle line:84%
I said, and chuckled
mysteriously.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:23.410 align:middle line:84%
And so I want to just show
you why I was doing that.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:27.690 align:middle line:84%
There are other kinds
of sounds out there.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.230 align:middle line:84%
So the first kind of sound
that I wanted to talk about

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.270 align:middle line:90%
are what are called ejectives.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:35.240 align:middle line:84%
And I have some sound files
that I'll play for you.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:37.640 align:middle line:84%
These were all
compiled and put online

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:43.310 align:middle line:84%
by the great phoneticist
Peter Ladefoged, who

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:44.570 align:middle line:90%
used to teach at UCLA.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:47.330 align:middle line:90%
He's no longer with us.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:52.550 align:middle line:84%
I'll put a link on the website
both to the particular classes

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.050 align:middle line:84%
of sounds that I'm
going to show you

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.120 align:middle line:84%
and to the larger
UCLA website, which

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:57.590 align:middle line:84%
has all kinds of
cool sound files

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.870 align:middle line:84%
on it that you can listen
to and amaze and annoy

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.470 align:middle line:90%
your friends with.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.770 align:middle line:84%
So the first type of sound
that I want to talk about

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:05.750 align:middle line:90%
are what are called ejectives.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.660 align:middle line:84%
There aren't languages of
Europe that have ejectives.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.210 align:middle line:84%
But they're quite popular in
the languages of North and South

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:12.870 align:middle line:90%
America.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:16.560 align:middle line:84%
And so it's worth
learning how to do them.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.970 align:middle line:84%
Let's start by making
an ejective "t."

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.330 align:middle line:84%
This is how you make
an ejective "t."

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:26.733 align:middle line:84%
So first, we'll practice like
this, you'll hold your breath.

00:11:26.733 --> 00:11:29.150 align:middle line:84%
Don't do it now because I have
other things to talk about.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.320 align:middle line:84%
But what you're
going to do is you're

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:31.880 align:middle line:84%
going to hold your
breath and try

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.050 align:middle line:90%
to make an audible "t." sound.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.075 align:middle line:90%
So go t, t, t.

00:11:37.075 --> 00:11:38.357 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: t, t, t.

00:11:38.357 --> 00:11:39.440 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK, good.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:42.400 align:middle line:84%
And then if you were actually
doing this in speech,

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.160 align:middle line:84%
you're not allowed to
stop and hold your breath.

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.880 align:middle line:90%
The idea is to get--

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:51.070 align:middle line:84%
well, so hold your breath,
make your ejective "t,"

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:55.347 align:middle line:84%
and then release into a
vowel, so go ah, ta, ah, ta.

00:11:55.347 --> 00:11:58.340 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Ah, ta, ah, ta.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.830 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Cool, you
guys sound very ejective.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:02.720 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, you can
do this with bilabial.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.010 align:middle line:84%
There are bilabial
ejective-- ah, pa, ah, pa--

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:12.860 align:middle line:84%
or velar ejective stops
like ah, ca, ah, ca.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.480 align:middle line:90%
Cool, excellent.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.890 align:middle line:84%
So we're all amateurs
at this, but the Lakota

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:21.270 align:middle line:90%
are professionals.

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:25.073 align:middle line:84%
So let's listen to a
Lakota speaker do this.

00:12:25.073 --> 00:12:25.740 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:26.335 align:middle line:90%


00:12:26.335 --> 00:12:28.085 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That's
an ejective velar.

00:12:28.085 --> 00:12:30.767 align:middle line:90%


00:12:30.767 --> 00:12:31.350 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:34.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: As opposed to,
here's her regular velar stop.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:35.220 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:36.887 align:middle line:90%


00:12:36.887 --> 00:12:37.470 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:39.143 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Here's a bilabial one.

00:12:39.143 --> 00:12:39.810 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:40.637 align:middle line:90%


00:12:40.637 --> 00:12:41.220 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:42.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
that's what ejectives

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:43.618 align:middle line:90%
sound like in the wild.

00:12:43.618 --> 00:12:44.250 align:middle line:90%
This is Lakota.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:47.850 align:middle line:84%
It's a language
still spoken, well,

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.200 align:middle line:90%
near North and South Dakota.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.245 align:middle line:84%
So there are the Lakotas, and
the Nakotas, and the Dakotas.

00:12:52.245 --> 00:12:53.370 align:middle line:90%
They all live in that area.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:54.630 align:middle line:90%
They're Siouan languages.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:55.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:55.782 align:middle line:90%
Oh, Raquel?

00:12:55.782 --> 00:12:57.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's the difference
between that and just

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:01.440 align:middle line:84%
the normal "k" sound
followed by the glottal stop?

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.690 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
it's very similar.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.530 align:middle line:84%
You're engaging
your-- so what you're

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.130 align:middle line:84%
doing when you make
these sounds is

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:16.920 align:middle line:90%
you are making a velar closure.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:21.600 align:middle line:84%
And then, you are also
making a glottal stop.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:24.450 align:middle line:84%
And then, you are
pushing with your glottis

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.840 align:middle line:90%
to make the velar closure open.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.600 align:middle line:84%
So you make your-- you
move your larynx in order

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.310 align:middle line:90%
to get the closure to open.

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.553 align:middle line:84%
And that's what gives
it this unique burst.

00:13:34.553 --> 00:13:35.220 align:middle line:90%
So you're right.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.925 align:middle line:84%
There is a glottal stop
involved when you're doing that.

00:13:37.925 --> 00:13:41.610 align:middle line:84%
We're at-- yeah, so when I
said that the flow of air

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:44.070 align:middle line:84%
is usually from
your lungs, here's

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.470 align:middle line:84%
the place where
it isn't exactly.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.840 align:middle line:84%
The air originally
came from your lungs.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, it originally
came from the world.

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.070 align:middle line:84%
It spent some time
in your lungs.

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:56.240 align:middle line:84%
And then, it got trapped
between this glottal stop

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:57.990 align:middle line:84%
at your larynx and the
closure that you're

00:13:57.990 --> 00:13:59.130 align:middle line:90%
making in your mouth.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.080 align:middle line:84%
And the flow of
air that causes--

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.710 align:middle line:84%
that's present as you're
opening the stop--

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:06.060 align:middle line:90%
is actually from your larynx.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:09.480 align:middle line:90%
It's not from your lungs.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:12.790 align:middle line:84%
It's not originally from your
lungs, if that makes sense.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:14.300 align:middle line:90%
So those are ejectives.

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.460 align:middle line:90%


00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.770 align:middle line:90%
Clicks.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.495 align:middle line:84%
I have some recordings of
clicks in here somewhere.

00:14:22.495 --> 00:14:23.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, there they are.

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:25.260 align:middle line:90%
These are Zulu clicks.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.710 align:middle line:84%
There are three main kinds
of clicks to think about.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:32.910 align:middle line:84%
And clicks, again, involve
a strange and cross--

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.250 align:middle line:84%
typologically unusual,
kind of, airflow.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:39.930 align:middle line:90%
So clicks are found in--

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.320 align:middle line:84%
so the languages with the
richest click inventories

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:47.850 align:middle line:84%
are the Khoisan languages
of places like Namibia.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.090 align:middle line:84%
And then, there are a
bunch of Bantu languages

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:56.460 align:middle line:84%
that also have clicks,
also in Africa, in theory

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.100 align:middle line:84%
because they've been in contact
with the Khoisan languages.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.320 align:middle line:84%
So clicks are really
cross-linguistically

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:02.610 align:middle line:90%
not terribly popular.

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:09.420 align:middle line:84%
They're only found in
that area and in one

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.420 align:middle line:84%
culturally restricted
language spoken by the Lardil.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:15.880 align:middle line:84%
So the Lardil have-- it's
almost a language game.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:20.700 align:middle line:84%
It's called Damin, which is
used after a certain initiation

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:21.270 align:middle line:90%
ceremony.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:22.560 align:middle line:84%
So there's a period
where you're not supposed

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:23.850 align:middle line:90%
to speak normal Lardil.

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:26.007 align:middle line:84%
And so you speak this
other thing instead,

00:15:26.007 --> 00:15:28.590 align:middle line:84%
which has all kinds of strange
sounds in it, including clicks.

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:29.340 align:middle line:90%
It's called Damin.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:31.720 align:middle line:84%
We may get a chance to
talk more about it later.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.330 align:middle line:84%
But for languages
that are not spoken

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:36.180 align:middle line:84%
under special cultural
circumstances,

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:38.850 align:middle line:84%
these African languages, the
Khoisan languages and the Bantu

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.767 align:middle line:84%
languages that have been
in contact with them,

00:15:40.767 --> 00:15:42.840 align:middle line:84%
are the only ones
that have clicks.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:44.910 align:middle line:84%
And I'm talking about
clicks now because they

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.370 align:middle line:90%
do involve an unusual airflow.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:50.580 align:middle line:84%
They involve making
a stop, a velar stop,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:54.750 align:middle line:84%
and then using your
tongue to sharply draw air

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:58.450 align:middle line:90%
into your mouth for a moment.

00:15:58.450 --> 00:15:59.790 align:middle line:90%
So what's a click?

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:02.070 align:middle line:90%
One click is the dental click.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:07.770 align:middle line:84%
It's the, kind of, sound you
make to criticize someone,

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.540 align:middle line:84%
to [TSK-ING] thing
that you sometimes--

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:11.040 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:11.868 align:middle line:90%
--see spelled tsk.

00:16:11.868 --> 00:16:18.870 align:middle line:84%
T-S-K. T-S-K. Yeah,
there is a lateral click.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:22.980 align:middle line:84%
Whenever I read about lateral
clicks, the description of them

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:26.730 align:middle line:84%
is always that it's the sound
you make to encourage a horse.

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.605 align:middle line:84%
I have never had to
encourage a horse--

00:16:28.605 --> 00:16:29.820 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:30.755 align:middle line:90%
--or discourage a--

00:16:30.755 --> 00:16:32.880 align:middle line:84%
I've never-- I haven't
interacted much with horses.

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:36.940 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not-- that description
doesn't help me a whole lot.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:39.960 align:middle line:84%
I gather it involves sucking
the sides of your tongue

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:41.996 align:middle line:90%
in from the sides of your mouth.

00:16:41.996 --> 00:16:43.121 align:middle line:90%
So you're going [CLICKING].

00:16:43.121 --> 00:16:45.285 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [CLICKING]

00:16:45.285 --> 00:16:47.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, I think
I hear some lateral clicks.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:48.560 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:49.460 align:middle line:90%


00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:52.800 align:middle line:84%
And then, there's
another click, which

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:55.767 align:middle line:84%
is sometimes called a palatal
click, which goes [CLUCKING]..

00:16:55.767 --> 00:16:56.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [CLUCKING]

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.072 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So you're--

00:16:58.072 --> 00:16:59.780 align:middle line:84%
oh, you guys are really
good at that one.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:00.280 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHING]

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:03.830 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so all three
of these clicks

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.420 align:middle line:84%
are present, for
example, in Zulu

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:08.660 align:middle line:84%
and in Xhosa, which is the
native language of Nelson

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:11.119 align:middle line:90%
Mandela.

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:14.359 align:middle line:84%
And here are some Zulu ones
that you can listen to.

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:16.723 align:middle line:84%
So we've got the
dental one first.

00:17:16.723 --> 00:17:17.390 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:18.817 align:middle line:90%


00:17:18.817 --> 00:17:19.400 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:21.991 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And
here's the lateral one.

00:17:21.991 --> 00:17:22.658 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:17:22.658 --> 00:17:23.962 align:middle line:90%


00:17:23.962 --> 00:17:24.545 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:24.545 --> 00:17:26.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And
the alveopalatal one.

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:26.847 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:17:26.847 --> 00:17:28.520 align:middle line:90%


00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:29.183 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:29.183 --> 00:17:31.100 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And as
you can see from this,

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:36.440 align:middle line:84%
Zulu draws distinctions
depending on, basically,

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:38.540 align:middle line:90%
how you release the stave.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:41.510 align:middle line:90%
So there are clicks--

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:43.910 align:middle line:84%
those clicks, the ones
I just played for you,

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:47.902 align:middle line:84%
involve a voiceless stop
that's not aspirated,

00:17:47.902 --> 00:17:50.360 align:middle line:84%
that doesn't have this puff of
air that I was talking about

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:51.867 align:middle line:84%
and that we will
talk about more.

00:17:51.867 --> 00:17:53.450 align:middle line:84%
I was talking about
when I was talking

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:55.940 align:middle line:90%
about "philosophy" and Greek.

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:58.550 align:middle line:84%
But you can also build
them on aspirated stops,

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:02.187 align:middle line:84%
or on voiced stops,
or even on nasals.

00:18:02.187 --> 00:18:04.520 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll just run through
all of the dental clicks.

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:05.720 align:middle line:90%
Here's the voiceless one again.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:06.387 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:06.387 --> 00:18:07.807 align:middle line:90%


00:18:07.807 --> 00:18:08.390 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:09.350 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: But
here, it is aspirated.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:10.200 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:10.797 align:middle line:90%


00:18:10.797 --> 00:18:11.380 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:12.338 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ta ga.

00:18:12.338 --> 00:18:15.080 align:middle line:84%
So there's air coming
out as you do this.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:16.040 align:middle line:90%
And here it is voiced.

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:17.340 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:18.050 align:middle line:90%


00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:19.440 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.065 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And
here, it is nasal.

00:18:21.065 --> 00:18:21.732 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:21.732 --> 00:18:23.107 align:middle line:90%


00:18:23.107 --> 00:18:23.690 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:25.250 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Whoa, that
hardly sounded like a click

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:25.750 align:middle line:90%
at all.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:27.736 align:middle line:84%
Here, let me try to
find another nasal one.

00:18:27.736 --> 00:18:28.450 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:29.387 align:middle line:90%


00:18:29.387 --> 00:18:29.970 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:31.553 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yep, that was clicky.

00:18:31.553 --> 00:18:32.250 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.080 align:middle line:90%
And here it is as a lateral.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.220 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.343 align:middle line:90%


00:18:36.343 --> 00:18:37.510 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: [CHUCKLING]

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:38.200 align:middle line:90%


00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:38.940 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.230 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Zulu
really does sound like that.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:41.730 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHING]

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:43.280 align:middle line:84%
We did a field
methods class on Zulu.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:45.300 align:middle line:84%
And it has this beautiful--
because it's tonal.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.460 align:middle line:84%
So it has all of this--
the intonation is just

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.190 align:middle line:90%
really pretty to listen to.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:51.967 align:middle line:84%
So clicks is another
kind of thing, not

00:18:51.967 --> 00:18:53.550 align:middle line:84%
all that popular
cross-linguistically.

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:54.270 align:middle line:90%
But it happens.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:55.800 align:middle line:90%
And they're magnificent.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:59.340 align:middle line:84%
The Khoisan languages
have even richer arrays

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:01.380 align:middle line:90%
of click inventories than this.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:05.510 align:middle line:84%
In particular, they also
have bilabial clicks.

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:07.340 align:middle line:84%
So they have
[CLICKING] as a sound.

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:09.807 align:middle line:84%
[CLICKING],, that
kind of a sound.

00:19:09.807 --> 00:19:12.530 align:middle line:84%
And they make all of
these distinctions

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:13.940 align:middle line:90%
among their clicks.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:16.400 align:middle line:84%
The result ends up being that
the Khoisan languages, if you

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.940 align:middle line:84%
count all of these clicks
as different consonants,

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:22.775 align:middle line:84%
the Khoisan languages have just
huge inventories of consonants

00:19:22.775 --> 00:19:24.650 align:middle line:84%
because they have all
these different clicks.

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:25.670 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:28.964 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are the accents
above the vowels indicative

00:19:28.964 --> 00:19:30.757 align:middle line:90%
of what direction the tone is?

00:19:30.757 --> 00:19:32.590 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
those are tone marks.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so the accents that
points down to the right

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:38.500 align:middle line:90%
are low-tones.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:40.875 align:middle line:84%
And the accents the point up
to the right are high-tones.

00:19:40.875 --> 00:19:42.580 align:middle line:90%
And that's all Zulu's got.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.470 align:middle line:84%
It's got high-tones
and low-tones.

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:47.440 align:middle line:84%
And then, you can have
long vowels that transition

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:50.950 align:middle line:84%
from a high-tone to a
low-tone or vice versa.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:54.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yep.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:56.940 align:middle line:90%
Clicks.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:57.900 align:middle line:90%
Implosives.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:19:59.640 align:middle line:90%
Let me find you some implosives.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.260 align:middle line:90%
They're around here somewhere.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:02.240 align:middle line:90%
Where were they?

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.410 align:middle line:90%
These are implosives.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:10.800 align:middle line:84%
Implosives, what implosives
are, are sounds where--

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.130 align:middle line:84%
so what you should do if you
want to make an implosive,

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:14.550 align:middle line:90%
ah...

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:18.090 align:middle line:84%
"b," for example, is you should
make your bilabial closure.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.250 align:middle line:84%
And then, you should
try to inhale.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, "bah,
bah, bah, bah"

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:25.110 align:middle line:90%
[pronounced with implosive "b"].

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:26.980 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, so
don't hurt yourself.

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:28.480 align:middle line:84%
But that's what
you're trying to do.

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:29.260 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.170 align:middle line:84%
And there are also
velar implosives,

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.130 align:middle line:84%
which you should
really be careful with.

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:33.167 align:middle line:84%
They're "ag ba, ag ba"
[pronounced with implosive

00:20:33.167 --> 00:20:33.667 align:middle line:90%
"g"].

00:20:33.667 --> 00:20:34.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:35.730 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Or "ah dah, ah dah"

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:36.104 align:middle line:90%
[pronounced with implosive "d"

00:20:36.104 --> 00:20:37.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.060 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.950 align:middle line:90%
So here's a native speaker.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:45.960 align:middle line:84%
So these are not all that
uncommon in languages of India.

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:49.230 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of
them in Indonesia.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:49.890 align:middle line:90%
Here are some.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:50.610 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:51.197 align:middle line:90%


00:20:51.197 --> 00:20:51.780 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:54.450 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
That's a bilabial one.

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:55.658 align:middle line:90%
Here's a retroflex one.

00:20:55.658 --> 00:20:56.325 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:20:56.325 --> 00:20:56.612 align:middle line:90%


00:20:56.612 --> 00:20:57.195 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:20:57.195 --> 00:20:57.536 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Dinu"
[pronounced with voiced

00:20:57.536 --> 00:20:58.065 align:middle line:90%
retroflex implosive "d"].

00:20:58.065 --> 00:20:58.565 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:20:58.565 --> 00:21:00.600 align:middle line:84%
So we can compare
his implosive one

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:01.793 align:middle line:90%
with the regular voiced one.

00:21:01.793 --> 00:21:03.210 align:middle line:84%
So here's the
implosive one again.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:03.877 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:21:03.877 --> 00:21:04.030 align:middle line:90%


00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:04.220 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:06.637 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, and
then, here it is not implosive.

00:21:06.637 --> 00:21:07.303 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:21:07.303 --> 00:21:07.470 align:middle line:90%


00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:08.160 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:10.150 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, and here's

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:11.650 align:middle line:84%
what it sounds like
when it's velar.

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:12.317 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:21:12.317 --> 00:21:12.357 align:middle line:90%


00:21:12.357 --> 00:21:12.940 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:14.530 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: As
opposed to the regular one.

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:15.197 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:21:15.197 --> 00:21:15.507 align:middle line:90%


00:21:15.507 --> 00:21:16.090 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:19.750 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
again, I'll put these files up.

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:25.098 align:middle line:84%
You can play with them at home,
amaze and annoy your friends.

00:21:25.098 --> 00:21:26.390 align:middle line:90%
No questions about any of that?

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:30.200 align:middle line:84%
So again, this has all been
about interesting air flows.

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:33.110 align:middle line:84%
So here's one where the flow
of air is not outward at all.

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:36.070 align:middle line:84%
It's going inward for just
a millisecond, yeah, trying

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:38.860 align:middle line:84%
to suck air into your body
instead of getting it out

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:40.660 align:middle line:90%
as you speak.

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:44.860 align:middle line:84%
There are-- so I don't
know of any languages that

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:48.070 align:middle line:84%
have, for example,
a class of vowels

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:50.920 align:middle line:84%
that you must make
while inhaling.

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:53.110 align:middle line:84%
So these are consonants
that you make

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:54.428 align:middle line:90%
while attempting to inhale.

00:21:54.428 --> 00:21:56.720 align:middle line:84%
That's the sense in which
the flow of air is different.

00:21:56.720 --> 00:21:59.320 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine a language
that had the vowel "a"

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:02.622 align:middle line:90%
and also the vowel [GASP].

00:22:02.622 --> 00:22:04.330 align:middle line:84%
I've never heard of
a language like that.

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:08.500 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that will
inhale for particular words

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:09.340 align:middle line:90%
or classes of words.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:15.190 align:middle line:84%
So I'm told that
in Finland, there's

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:18.400 align:middle line:84%
an expression that
basically means "yes."

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:22.480 align:middle line:84%
But it's used only
by middle-aged women.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.010 align:middle line:90%
And it involves inhaling.

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:26.530 align:middle line:90%
You say, [GASP].

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:29.180 align:middle line:84%
And that marks you as a
middle-aged Finnish woman.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:29.680 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.245 align:middle line:84%
So if you're ever
attempting to sound

00:22:31.245 --> 00:22:32.620 align:middle line:84%
like a middle-aged
Finnish woman,

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:34.535 align:middle line:90%
you should use that a lot, yeah.

00:22:34.535 --> 00:22:36.160 align:middle line:84%
But I've never heard
of a language that

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.830 align:middle line:84%
had-- there are languages that
have a series of nasal vowels,

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:39.470 align:middle line:90%
for example.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:40.360 align:middle line:84%
But there aren't
languages that have

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:41.590 align:middle line:90%
a series of inhaled vowels.

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:44.080 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't happen,
no, as far as I know.

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:48.198 align:middle line:90%


00:22:48.198 --> 00:22:50.490 align:middle line:84%
And then trills, these don't
involve a special airflow.

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:53.150 align:middle line:84%
But I wanted to talk about them
partly because they came up

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:53.650 align:middle line:90%
in class.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:53.730 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [TRILLING]

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:55.260 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, like that.

00:22:55.260 --> 00:23:00.210 align:middle line:84%
So trills involve getting
some type of-- some part

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:04.200 align:middle line:90%
of your vocal tract to flutter.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:07.890 align:middle line:84%
And there are ones that
are comparatively famous,

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:09.615 align:middle line:90%
like the so-called rolled "r."

00:23:09.615 --> 00:23:12.833 align:middle line:90%


00:23:12.833 --> 00:23:15.250 align:middle line:84%
These are some examples that
are going to sound like that.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:16.042 align:middle line:90%
This is a language.

00:23:16.042 --> 00:23:17.700 align:middle line:84%
This is a Dravidian
language called

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:22.360 align:middle line:84%
Toda that has dental trills
and also postalveolar trills.

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:24.650 align:middle line:90%
So you can listen to those.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:25.610 align:middle line:90%
Sure we can.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:27.080 align:middle line:90%
Come on.

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.755 align:middle line:90%
Wake up, website.

00:23:29.755 --> 00:23:30.380 align:middle line:90%
I take it back.

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:31.992 align:middle line:90%
We can't listen to those.

00:23:31.992 --> 00:23:33.200 align:middle line:90%
Why can't we listen to those?

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.270 align:middle line:84%
I was listening
to them just now.

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:36.250 align:middle line:90%
What is your deal?

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:36.750 align:middle line:90%
Never mind.

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:37.875 align:middle line:90%
We'll go to this other one.

00:23:37.875 --> 00:23:40.680 align:middle line:90%


00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:43.700 align:middle line:84%
So the rolled "r"
the [PURRING] sound,

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:46.550 align:middle line:84%
that's the alveolar
trill, where you're

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:48.920 align:middle line:84%
holding your tongue fairly
close to your alveolar ridge,

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:51.950 align:middle line:84%
and getting the air to flow,
and holding your tongue in such

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:55.610 align:middle line:84%
a way that your tongue flutters
in the breeze as it goes by.

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:56.730 align:middle line:90%
English doesn't have that.

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:00.080 align:middle line:84%
But if you want to learn to
speak Italian, or Spanish,

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:02.870 align:middle line:84%
or a variety of
languages of Australia,

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:06.440 align:middle line:84%
you need to learn how
to roll your "r"s.

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:10.610 align:middle line:84%
It's possible to be physically
incapable of doing that.

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:13.340 align:middle line:84%
In fact, so for example, there
are Italians who cannot roll

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:15.320 align:middle line:90%
their "r"s.

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:16.880 align:middle line:90%
But it's not all that common.

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:19.550 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's the kind of thing
that is a speech impediment

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:21.980 align:middle line:84%
if your native language
has rolled "r"s.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:23.660 align:middle line:84%
If your native language
is English then

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:25.820 align:middle line:90%
you're off the hook.

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:28.760 align:middle line:84%
There are also languages
that have uvular trills.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:30.440 align:middle line:84%
That's one possible
pronunciation

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.012 align:middle line:84%
of the French "r''
or the German "r."

00:24:33.012 --> 00:24:35.150 align:middle line:90%
[PURRING IN BACK OF MOUTH]

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:37.220 align:middle line:84%
I can only do a uvular
trill with my head

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:40.550 align:middle line:90%
tilted back slightly.

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:43.070 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, that's, again,
getting your uvula

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:44.063 align:middle line:90%
to move in the breeze.

00:24:44.063 --> 00:24:44.855 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Gargling.

00:24:44.855 --> 00:24:45.500 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm sorry.

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:46.500 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Like gargling.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:47.803 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Like gargling.

00:24:47.803 --> 00:24:49.220 align:middle line:84%
And then, there
are languages that

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:51.890 align:middle line:84%
have bilabial trills, which was
what Raquel was doing for us.

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:55.540 align:middle line:84%
Let me see if I can
get these to come out.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:58.490 align:middle line:90%
Ah, come on.

00:24:58.490 --> 00:24:58.990 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:24:58.990 --> 00:24:59.440 align:middle line:90%
The UCLA website.

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:00.107 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:25:00.107 --> 00:25:00.190 align:middle line:90%


00:25:00.190 --> 00:25:00.400 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:00.850 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: There we go.

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:01.517 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:25:01.517 --> 00:25:01.537 align:middle line:90%


00:25:01.537 --> 00:25:02.120 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:02.950 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
that's the sound that

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:04.150 align:middle line:90%
begins with a bilabial trill.

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:04.633 align:middle line:90%
[BLOWING]

00:25:04.633 --> 00:25:05.116 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [BLOWING]

00:25:05.116 --> 00:25:06.760 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: [BLOWING] Yeah,
you're better at it than me.

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:07.427 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:25:07.427 --> 00:25:07.927 align:middle line:90%


00:25:07.927 --> 00:25:08.573 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:25:08.573 --> 00:25:10.240 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
here's another one.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:10.907 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:25:10.907 --> 00:25:13.947 align:middle line:90%


00:25:13.947 --> 00:25:14.530 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:16.510 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, so this is Kele,

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:21.520 align:middle line:84%
it's a language spoken in Gabon,
and Titan, which is, I believe,

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:22.903 align:middle line:90%
a Polynesian language.

00:25:22.903 --> 00:25:24.820 align:middle line:84%
These are languages that
have bilabial trills.

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:25.800 align:middle line:90%
Is that a question?

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:26.950 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Question.

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:31.960 align:middle line:84%
Remind me why rolled "r"s are
called "rolled 'r's" again.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.210 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That's
the-- so in linguistics,

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:35.543 align:middle line:90%
they aren't usually called that.

00:25:35.543 --> 00:25:37.510 align:middle line:90%
They're called alveolar trills.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:39.040 align:middle line:84%
We call them rolled
"r"s, I think,

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:41.890 align:middle line:84%
because they sound a little
bit like a drum roll.

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:43.675 align:middle line:84%
It's like there's
a repetitive sound.

00:25:43.675 --> 00:25:46.540 align:middle line:84%
[PURRING] You're
repeatedly striking

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:48.585 align:middle line:90%
the roof of your mouth.

00:25:48.585 --> 00:25:50.710 align:middle line:84%
That's what I always thought
that term referred to.

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:51.370 align:middle line:90%
But as I say.

00:25:51.370 --> 00:25:52.972 align:middle line:90%
It's not a linguistic term.

00:25:52.972 --> 00:25:55.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Remind me why
drums rolls [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:58.890 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
that's a good question.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:00.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, because they
sound like rolled "r"s.

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:03.340 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:05.560 align:middle line:84%
I guess they sound like
something is rolling

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:09.465 align:middle line:84%
and repeatedly-- so they involve
a repeated strike of something.

00:26:09.465 --> 00:26:10.840 align:middle line:84%
So if you have
something rolling,

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:14.020 align:middle line:84%
bumping down a
staircase or something,

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:15.920 align:middle line:84%
then it might sound a
little bit like that.

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:16.462 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:26:16.462 --> 00:26:17.860 align:middle line:90%
I am now making things up.

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:21.290 align:middle line:84%
I will try to flag it
when I'm making things up.

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:23.262 align:middle line:90%
But yes, I don't know.

00:26:23.262 --> 00:26:24.220 align:middle line:90%
That's a good question.

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:26.262 align:middle line:84%
You should go look it up
in the OED or something.

00:26:26.262 --> 00:26:27.960 align:middle line:90%
Maybe they'll have a theory.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:29.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, other questions?

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:33.340 align:middle line:90%


00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:34.340 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:37.850 align:middle line:84%
So enough of the
bilabial trills.

00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:41.180 align:middle line:90%


00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:45.950 align:middle line:84%
And so yeah, as I say, I'll
put the UCLA sound files up

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:51.350 align:middle line:84%
there so that you can
play around with them.

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:52.340 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry.

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:55.040 align:middle line:90%
I spoke too soon.

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:59.000 align:middle line:84%
So we've been talking about
sounds for a little while.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:04.310 align:middle line:84%
And I've been encouraging you to
try to make the strange sounds.

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:06.890 align:middle line:84%
You've all been clicking
at me and trilling at me.

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:08.430 align:middle line:90%
It's been great.

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:12.770 align:middle line:84%
But there is another way
to study speech sounds.

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:15.860 align:middle line:84%
And I just wanted to
show you how it works.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:16.610 align:middle line:90%
I hope this works.

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:18.350 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

00:27:18.350 --> 00:27:22.220 align:middle line:84%
It's possible to make what
are called spectrograms.

00:27:22.220 --> 00:27:24.260 align:middle line:84%
And maybe what I'll do
is just show you one.

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:27.185 align:middle line:84%
And that'll make it easier
to talk about what they are.

00:27:27.185 --> 00:27:28.685 align:middle line:84%
Let's see if I can
get this to work.

00:27:28.685 --> 00:27:39.236 align:middle line:90%


00:27:39.236 --> 00:27:39.736 align:middle line:90%
You.

00:27:39.736 --> 00:27:43.480 align:middle line:90%


00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:46.720 align:middle line:90%
Let's see if we can see that.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:47.320 align:middle line:90%
There.

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.680 align:middle line:90%
That's a spectrogram.

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:52.820 align:middle line:90%
Let me Zoom in on it.

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:55.260 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, there we go.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.230 align:middle line:84%
And I can zoom in on
it a little further.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:27:58.550 align:middle line:90%
It isn't all vowels, is it?

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:01.090 align:middle line:90%


00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:04.330 align:middle line:84%
So this is a program
called Praat.

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:05.410 align:middle line:90%
It's a freeware program.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:07.300 align:middle line:90%
I'll put it up online.

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:09.730 align:middle line:84%
It's something that allows
you to make spectrograms.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.710 align:middle line:90%
As you can see, it's very easy.

00:28:11.710 --> 00:28:13.660 align:middle line:84%
It's a pretty easy
program to use.

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:16.000 align:middle line:84%
I'll put up-- it's
a program that's

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:17.270 align:middle line:90%
been around for a long time.

00:28:17.270 --> 00:28:20.110 align:middle line:84%
It was developed by linguists
and not by software engineers.

00:28:20.110 --> 00:28:21.190 align:middle line:90%
And you can tell.

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:23.608 align:middle line:90%
So it's a little bit--

00:28:23.608 --> 00:28:25.150 align:middle line:84%
the interface is a
little bit clunky.

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:28.492 align:middle line:84%
But I'll put up some
suggestions about how to use it.

00:28:28.492 --> 00:28:30.950 align:middle line:84%
And I encourage you to download
it and play around with it.

00:28:30.950 --> 00:28:32.770 align:middle line:90%
It's fun.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:35.920 align:middle line:84%
What's going on here, the thing
on the bottom of this window

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:38.950 align:middle line:90%
is the spectrogram.

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:42.250 align:middle line:84%
And it's a spectrogram showing
me making those vowel sounds,

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:46.180 align:middle line:84%
"a," "i," and "u,"
transitioning between them.

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:48.778 align:middle line:84%
I think I might be able to play
what we're looking at here.

00:28:48.778 --> 00:28:49.445 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:28:49.445 --> 00:28:49.497 align:middle line:90%


00:28:49.497 --> 00:28:50.080 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:52.000 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, so I didn't

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:53.770 align:middle line:90%
keep very much of the "u."

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:57.400 align:middle line:84%
That we can think just
about the "a" and the "i."

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:01.120 align:middle line:84%
What you're doing when you
are making vowels especially,

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:03.190 align:middle line:84%
but actually when you're
speaking generally,

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:05.870 align:middle line:84%
is your vocal
cords, in this case,

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:07.930 align:middle line:90%
my vocal chords were vibrating.

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:10.720 align:middle line:84%
They were making
the air vibrate.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:12.490 align:middle line:84%
There's this column
of air that comes up

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:14.710 align:middle line:90%
through my vocal tract.

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:16.160 align:middle line:90%
They're making that air vibrate.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:19.150 align:middle line:84%
And then, those vibrations are
interacting with other things

00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:21.160 align:middle line:90%
I'm doing with my vocal tract.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:24.230 align:middle line:84%
They're bouncing off the
sides of my vocal tract.

00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:26.350 align:middle line:84%
And as I change the
shape of my vocal tract,

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:29.530 align:middle line:84%
I change what those
vibrations are doing.

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:32.560 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's a
complicated phenomenon.

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:36.490 align:middle line:84%
You end up creating this
complicated waveform, which

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:40.570 align:middle line:84%
you can do Fourier
analysis of and analyze it

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:44.320 align:middle line:84%
as a waveform that has
a bunch of regular sine

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:48.220 align:middle line:84%
waves of various
frequencies and amplitudes

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:49.480 align:middle line:90%
that are contributing to it.

00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:52.240 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and this is what you do
when you do a Fourier analysis,

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:53.410 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:57.130 align:middle line:84%
And what the spectrogram
does is mark for you

00:29:57.130 --> 00:30:00.310 align:middle line:84%
the frequencies that are making
the greatest contribution

00:30:00.310 --> 00:30:04.840 align:middle line:84%
to the general
complicated air waveform.

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:07.400 align:middle line:90%
There are the darker parts.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:10.570 align:middle line:84%
So these dark bands
that you're seeing

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:13.000 align:middle line:90%
are what are called formants.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.000 align:middle line:84%
And they are the things
that are contributing

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.670 align:middle line:90%
the most to the sound.

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:23.020 align:middle line:84%
As you can see, they're shifting
as I shift from "ah" to "ee."

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:26.500 align:middle line:84%
So this formant
down-- no, let's see.

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:31.240 align:middle line:90%
Can we see my cursor on this?

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.390 align:middle line:84%
Yes, this formant
right here, you

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:36.710 align:middle line:84%
can see as I shift from
"ah" to "ee," it goes up.

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:37.210 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:39.745 align:middle line:84%
And this formant up here, as
I shift from "ah" to "ee,"

00:30:39.745 --> 00:30:41.950 align:middle line:90%
it dips.

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:45.880 align:middle line:84%
So how far apart the
formants are from each other

00:30:45.880 --> 00:30:49.060 align:middle line:84%
is what distinguishes the
vowels from each other.

00:30:49.060 --> 00:30:51.850 align:middle line:84%
You're hearing this
difference in formants.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:53.710 align:middle line:90%
You can do this for yourself.

00:30:53.710 --> 00:30:55.180 align:middle line:84%
Here, I'm going
to do another one.

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:58.924 align:middle line:90%


00:30:58.924 --> 00:30:59.730 align:middle line:90%
Hang on.

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:05.486 align:middle line:90%


00:31:05.486 --> 00:31:05.986 align:middle line:90%
"Wah."

00:31:05.986 --> 00:31:10.490 align:middle line:90%


00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:11.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so here's another one.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:14.860 align:middle line:90%


00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:17.800 align:middle line:84%
I didn't get a very good
recording of that one.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:18.580 align:middle line:90%
Try that again.

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:24.040 align:middle line:90%


00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:24.540 align:middle line:90%
"Wah."

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:33.870 align:middle line:90%


00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:34.370 align:middle line:90%
Well.

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:35.037 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:31:35.037 --> 00:31:36.039 align:middle line:90%


00:31:36.039 --> 00:31:36.622 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:31:36.622 --> 00:31:37.796 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Hmm.

00:31:37.796 --> 00:31:38.296 align:middle line:90%
Hmm.

00:31:38.296 --> 00:31:40.046 align:middle line:84%
That's not doing what
I'm expecting it to.

00:31:40.046 --> 00:31:40.570 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:44.668 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I think I see
what it's doing.

00:31:44.668 --> 00:31:47.210 align:middle line:84%
It has to do with the fact that
the microphone in my computer

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:48.830 align:middle line:90%
is lousy.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:51.523 align:middle line:84%
You can see the formant
shifting for this one.

00:31:51.523 --> 00:31:53.690 align:middle line:84%
Actually, you can't see
them shifting all that much.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:55.760 align:middle line:84%
But you can hear a
formant shifting.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:32:01.030 align:middle line:90%
If you do the shift--

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:01.740 align:middle line:90%
oh, no, wait.

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:04.240 align:middle line:84%
I think I can think of something
that will show this better.

00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:05.032 align:middle line:90%
Hang on just a sec.

00:32:05.032 --> 00:32:05.680 align:middle line:90%
One more thing.

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:13.980 align:middle line:90%


00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:14.490 align:middle line:90%
Stop.

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:14.790 align:middle line:90%
Stop.

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:15.290 align:middle line:90%
Stop.

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:17.310 align:middle line:90%
Let's try "ee-oo."

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:22.050 align:middle line:90%


00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:22.550 align:middle line:90%
There we go.

00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:23.690 align:middle line:90%
That's better.

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:26.110 align:middle line:84%
So here, you can
clearly see a formant

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:29.470 align:middle line:84%
going from up here to
down here as I transition

00:32:29.470 --> 00:32:30.558 align:middle line:90%
from "ee" to "oo."

00:32:30.558 --> 00:32:31.600 align:middle line:90%
Is everybody seeing that?

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:32.720 align:middle line:90%
I'll play it again.

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:33.394 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:32:33.394 --> 00:32:33.894 align:middle line:90%


00:32:33.894 --> 00:32:35.307 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:32:35.307 --> 00:32:37.515 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I sound
like a dolphin or something.

00:32:37.515 --> 00:32:39.000 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:39.467 align:middle line:90%


00:32:39.467 --> 00:32:40.050 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.090 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, but as I switch

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:46.005 align:middle line:84%
from the "ee" to the "oo," you
can see a formant descending,

00:32:46.005 --> 00:32:48.030 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:49.650 align:middle line:90%
And you can hear that.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.177 align:middle line:90%
If you do the--

00:32:51.177 --> 00:32:53.010 align:middle line:84%
so this is something
that we were doing back

00:32:53.010 --> 00:32:54.385 align:middle line:84%
when I was trying
to convince you

00:32:54.385 --> 00:32:56.880 align:middle line:84%
that vowels were worth
categorizing, that the switch

00:32:56.880 --> 00:33:00.180 align:middle line:84%
from "ee" to "oo" involves your
tongue going from being front

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:03.330 align:middle line:84%
to being back, and also your
lips going from being not

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:05.190 align:middle line:90%
rounded to being rounded, yeah.

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:15.820 align:middle line:84%
And if you whisper that to
yourself, so go [SLURPING],,

00:33:15.820 --> 00:33:19.730 align:middle line:84%
you might be hearing
something descending in pitch.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:20.750 align:middle line:90%
Are you hearing that?

00:33:20.750 --> 00:33:22.710 align:middle line:90%
Just go [SLURPING].

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:27.068 align:middle line:90%


00:33:27.068 --> 00:33:28.360 align:middle line:90%
And that's what you're hearing.

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:29.560 align:middle line:90%
It's that formant.

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:32.590 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, by whispering,
you're getting

00:33:32.590 --> 00:33:33.610 align:middle line:90%
that to leap out at you.

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:36.220 align:middle line:84%
And you can hear it
descending in pitch.

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:37.960 align:middle line:90%
Why is it descending in pitch?

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:39.530 align:middle line:84%
If you think about
what you're doing,

00:33:39.530 --> 00:33:42.160 align:middle line:84%
think about the inside
of your vocal tract.

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:48.820 align:middle line:84%
As you go from "ee" to "oo,"
let's talk first about "oo."

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:54.280 align:middle line:84%
So "ooh" ["u"] is a
high, back rounded vowel.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:55.535 align:middle line:90%
Was any of that surprising?

00:33:55.535 --> 00:33:56.035 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:56.035 --> 00:33:57.220 align:middle line:90%
"Oo."

00:33:57.220 --> 00:33:59.980 align:middle line:84%
So your tongue is
high and bunched up

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:01.300 align:middle line:90%
in the back of your mouth.

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:02.710 align:middle line:90%
And your lips are rounded.

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:03.790 align:middle line:90%
Ooh.

00:34:03.790 --> 00:34:06.400 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that means
that so what you're

00:34:06.400 --> 00:34:08.170 align:middle line:84%
doing when you're
making vowels is

00:34:08.170 --> 00:34:11.920 align:middle line:84%
you're taking this column there
that's in your vocal tract.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:14.380 align:middle line:84%
And you're, depending
on the vowel,

00:34:14.380 --> 00:34:18.639 align:middle line:84%
making a partition somewhere
that isn't complete.

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:20.500 align:middle line:84%
You're not blocking
off part of the tract.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:22.630 align:middle line:84%
But you're putting
your tongue in the way.

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:26.650 align:middle line:84%
So you're making part of
that column of air narrower.

00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:28.310 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:30.699 align:middle line:84%
So for a high back
rounded vowel,

00:34:30.699 --> 00:34:33.790 align:middle line:84%
by bunching your tongue up
toward the back of your mouth,

00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:37.840 align:middle line:84%
you're making not a complete
obstruction of the flow of air.

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:40.120 align:middle line:84%
There's still space for
the air to flow past it.

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:43.100 align:middle line:84%
But you're making it
narrower back there, yeah.

00:34:43.100 --> 00:34:46.900 align:middle line:84%
And when you make an "oo" by
making a high back gesture

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:49.449 align:middle line:84%
with your tongue and
by rounding your lips,

00:34:49.449 --> 00:34:55.960 align:middle line:84%
you're creating a compartment
for your vocal tract, which

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:58.330 align:middle line:90%
is as large as you can make it.

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:00.760 align:middle line:84%
Your lips are out
there in front.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:03.340 align:middle line:84%
And your tongue is as
far back as it goes.

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:05.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, does that make sense?

00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:09.370 align:middle line:84%
You're compartmentalizing
off that top space

00:35:09.370 --> 00:35:13.240 align:middle line:90%
of your vocal tract.

00:35:13.240 --> 00:35:16.180 align:middle line:84%
And you're making that
space as large as it can be.

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:19.630 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to "ee" ["i"],
where your lips are not rounded

00:35:19.630 --> 00:35:21.880 align:middle line:84%
and your tongue is toward
the front of your mouth.

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:24.700 align:middle line:84%
So that makes that space
that you created for ooh.

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:26.530 align:middle line:90%
That makes that space collapse.

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:29.110 align:middle line:84%
It makes that space as
small as it can possibly

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:30.968 align:middle line:84%
be because now your
tongue is shoved up

00:35:30.968 --> 00:35:32.260 align:middle line:90%
toward the front of your mouth.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:33.760 align:middle line:90%
And your lips are not rounded.

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:35.620 align:middle line:84%
The space between your
tongue and your lips

00:35:35.620 --> 00:35:37.075 align:middle line:90%
is now as small as it gets.

00:35:37.075 --> 00:35:38.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:42.460 align:middle line:84%
And that's why the
transition from "ee" to "ooh"

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:45.040 align:middle line:84%
involves something
descending in pitch.

00:35:45.040 --> 00:35:47.710 align:middle line:84%
It's because you're going
from a very small compartment

00:35:47.710 --> 00:35:49.810 align:middle line:84%
with a little bit of
vibration inside it

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:53.590 align:middle line:84%
to a large compartment with
the same amount of vibration

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:55.270 align:middle line:84%
inside it because
your vocal cords are

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:57.160 align:middle line:90%
vibrating at a constant rate.

00:35:57.160 --> 00:35:59.230 align:middle line:84%
We're not-- you're not
fooling around with pitch.

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:00.320 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:04.320 align:middle line:84%
So as you switch from a small
compartment to a large one,

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:07.390 align:middle line:84%
one component of the very
complicated things that make up

00:36:07.390 --> 00:36:10.780 align:middle line:84%
this complicated waveform
is descending in pitch

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:13.480 align:middle line:84%
because you're creating
this little space that's

00:36:13.480 --> 00:36:16.210 align:middle line:84%
getting larger, and by putting
the same amount of energy

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:20.020 align:middle line:84%
to it, the molecules of
air are not moving as fast.

00:36:20.020 --> 00:36:22.150 align:middle line:84%
That's what we hear
as a lower pitch.

00:36:22.150 --> 00:36:24.050 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:26.440 align:middle line:84%
It's a way of talking
about this, anyway.

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:30.700 align:middle line:84%
So encourage you to
fool around with Praat.

00:36:30.700 --> 00:36:33.100 align:middle line:84%
Let me just show
you some consonants

00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:38.770 align:middle line:84%
so that you can see what
else you can do with Praat.

00:36:38.770 --> 00:36:40.015 align:middle line:90%
We'll try another one here.

00:36:40.015 --> 00:36:46.800 align:middle line:90%


00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:47.460 align:middle line:90%
"Bab.

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:48.190 align:middle line:90%
Dad.

00:36:48.190 --> 00:36:48.690 align:middle line:90%
Gag."

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:55.370 align:middle line:90%


00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:56.270 align:middle line:90%
View that one.

00:36:56.270 --> 00:36:59.120 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:03.590 align:middle line:90%


00:37:03.590 --> 00:37:04.610 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:07.235 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So here, I got
the same vowel all three times.

00:37:07.235 --> 00:37:10.652 align:middle line:84%
And you can see these nice
clear bars in the middle.

00:37:10.652 --> 00:37:12.860 align:middle line:84%
The difference has to do
with what the consonants are

00:37:12.860 --> 00:37:15.770 align:middle line:90%
on either side of the vowel.

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:21.030 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that
this representation,

00:37:21.030 --> 00:37:23.780 align:middle line:84%
this spectrogram, allows
us to concentrate on,

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:27.050 align:middle line:84%
if you think about
it, how do you

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:32.120 align:middle line:84%
tell the difference
between "bab" and "gag"?

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:36.740 align:middle line:84%
So we know articulatorially
what the difference is.

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:38.900 align:middle line:84%
The difference is
that, for "bab," you're

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:40.550 align:middle line:90%
making a bilabial stop.

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:43.970 align:middle line:84%
And for "gag," you're
making a velar stop.

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:45.500 align:middle line:84%
But when you're
hearing people make

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:48.710 align:middle line:84%
those sounds, how are you
hearing what the difference is

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:50.090 align:middle line:90%
between a B and a G?

00:37:50.090 --> 00:37:52.850 align:middle line:84%
Bear in mind that B
and G are both stops.

00:37:52.850 --> 00:37:55.250 align:middle line:84%
The flow of air
is stopped, yeah,

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:57.480 align:middle line:90%
while you're making the sound.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:37:59.450 align:middle line:84%
So while you're
making the sound,

00:37:59.450 --> 00:38:01.820 align:middle line:90%
you're not hearing things.

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:04.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:07.050 align:middle line:84%
The way you distinguish
"bab" from "gag"

00:38:07.050 --> 00:38:11.850 align:middle line:84%
is by looking at what it does
to the vowel that's in between.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.660 align:middle line:84%
You can see that these
formants for the "ah" vowel,

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.343 align:middle line:84%
and remember that the vowel was
the same in all three cases,

00:38:18.343 --> 00:38:20.010 align:middle line:84%
it's especially clear
for this last one.

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:21.210 align:middle line:90%
Here's "gag."

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:24.630 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that the
formants, this format

00:38:24.630 --> 00:38:26.910 align:middle line:90%
is smile-shaped.

00:38:26.910 --> 00:38:28.620 align:middle line:84%
And this formant
is frown-shaped.

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:33.180 align:middle line:84%
This one goes down as it
goes close to the stop.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:35.610 align:middle line:84%
And this one goes up as
it goes close to the top.

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:39.030 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to the bilabial,
which is, if anything,

00:38:39.030 --> 00:38:42.750 align:middle line:90%
slightly down on both sides.

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:45.860 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the things that
makes speech perception so hard

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:50.330 align:middle line:84%
and that makes it so hard to get
machines to understand speech.

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:52.760 align:middle line:84%
It's a problem that's
more or less solved.

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:57.770 align:middle line:84%
But it's a hard problem
because your speech is not just

00:38:57.770 --> 00:39:00.410 align:middle line:90%
a sequence of sounds.

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:04.580 align:middle line:84%
It's a sequence of sounds
that all influence each other.

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:07.580 align:middle line:84%
It's a mess in
other words, yeah?

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:11.720 align:middle line:84%
And the best cues for what sound
you made at a particular point

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:13.610 align:middle line:84%
might not be at
that point at all.

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:16.020 align:middle line:84%
They might be on
neighboring points.

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:19.290 align:middle line:84%
That's how you find out what
sound you're looking at.

00:39:19.290 --> 00:39:21.470 align:middle line:84%
And that's what makes
speech perception hard.

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:25.430 align:middle line:90%
It's also part of why--

00:39:25.430 --> 00:39:29.510 align:middle line:84%
so there are generalizations
about the kinds of syllables

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:32.570 align:middle line:90%
that we find in the world.

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:35.300 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty of
languages out there

00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:40.230 align:middle line:84%
in which it's OK for a
syllable to start with a stop.

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:43.250 align:middle line:84%
Let's say you can have
a syllable like "pa."

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:44.750 align:middle line:84%
That's an English
word more or less.

00:39:44.750 --> 00:39:48.500 align:middle line:84%
It's a word for father,
"pa," but in which

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:53.780 align:middle line:84%
it's not OK to have a P
followed by a fricative and then

00:39:53.780 --> 00:39:54.770 align:middle line:90%
a vowel.

00:39:54.770 --> 00:39:57.950 align:middle line:84%
So you don't have syllables
like "psah," in English,

00:39:57.950 --> 00:40:00.873 align:middle line:90%
or "pfah," yeah, or "pkah."

00:40:00.873 --> 00:40:02.540 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine that
there are languages

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:03.930 align:middle line:90%
that allow syllables like that.

00:40:03.930 --> 00:40:06.980 align:middle line:84%
But if a language is going
to restrict its syllables,

00:40:06.980 --> 00:40:08.960 align:middle line:84%
if it's only going to
have one of these kinds,

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:10.670 align:middle line:84%
it's only going
to have this kind.

00:40:10.670 --> 00:40:12.620 align:middle line:84%
Or to put it another
way, there are languages

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:13.700 align:middle line:90%
that have both of these.

00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:15.830 align:middle line:84%
And there are languages
that only have this.

00:40:15.830 --> 00:40:19.370 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any languages
that only have this, yeah?

00:40:19.370 --> 00:40:21.080 align:middle line:90%
And we can see why.

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:24.890 align:middle line:84%
The crucial cue to what,
kind of, stop you're hearing,

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:27.420 align:middle line:90%
it comes from the vowel.

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:31.160 align:middle line:84%
So if you put a fricative in
between the stop and the vowel,

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:34.250 align:middle line:84%
you deprive yourself of one of
the big cues for figuring out

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:36.000 align:middle line:84%
what, kind of, stop
you're listening to.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:39.800 align:middle line:84%
And so it makes sense
that we get this type

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:40.765 align:middle line:90%
of syllable everywhere.

00:40:40.765 --> 00:40:42.140 align:middle line:84%
And the other type
of syllable is

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:43.940 align:middle line:84%
only-- has a higher
degree of difficulty,

00:40:43.940 --> 00:40:45.810 align:middle line:84%
basically, as far
as perception goes.

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:47.210 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph.

00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:51.003 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What effect does that
have on [INAUDIBLE] especially.

00:40:51.003 --> 00:40:51.920 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:55.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: For one example,
where it's perfectly OK

00:40:55.130 --> 00:40:56.422 align:middle line:90%
to string three stops in a row.

00:40:56.422 --> 00:40:57.297 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

00:40:57.297 --> 00:40:58.490 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Or at least two.

00:40:58.490 --> 00:40:59.407 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:40:59.407 --> 00:41:01.522 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
then you can just--

00:41:01.522 --> 00:41:02.480 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Right.

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:05.450 align:middle line:84%
So I've shown you the
easiest way to figure out

00:41:05.450 --> 00:41:06.980 align:middle line:84%
what a stop is,
which is by looking

00:41:06.980 --> 00:41:08.450 align:middle line:84%
at its effect on
the nearby vowel.

00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:09.470 align:middle line:90%
But you're absolutely right.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:10.887 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
out there that

00:41:10.887 --> 00:41:14.360 align:middle line:84%
have strings of stops, strings
of stops and fricatives.

00:41:14.360 --> 00:41:15.620 align:middle line:90%
It absolutely happens.

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:18.815 align:middle line:84%
Russian has words
like [RUSSIAN] it's

00:41:18.815 --> 00:41:20.990 align:middle line:84%
got of a whole string
of stops and fricatives

00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:25.490 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of a word,
a beginning of a syllable.

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:29.318 align:middle line:84%
The most famous examples
of this are languages--

00:41:29.318 --> 00:41:30.860 align:middle line:84%
there are two kinds
of languages that

00:41:30.860 --> 00:41:32.300 align:middle line:90%
are especially famous for this.

00:41:32.300 --> 00:41:37.160 align:middle line:84%
There's a Salish language
called Bella Coola.

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:40.130 align:middle line:84%
The Salish languages are spoken
in the Pacific Northwest.

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:42.290 align:middle line:84%
Bella Coola, there's only
one word of Bella Coola

00:41:42.290 --> 00:41:43.340 align:middle line:90%
that I know.

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:44.360 align:middle line:90%
And it's this.

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:48.680 align:middle line:84%
So this is a uvular fricative,
a voiceless uvular fricative.

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:52.070 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to try
to pronounce that?

00:41:52.070 --> 00:41:53.834 align:middle line:90%
It's something like sauce.

00:41:53.834 --> 00:41:54.758 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:54.758 --> 00:41:57.050 align:middle line:84%
It's the only thing I know
how to say in Bella Coola.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:58.640 align:middle line:84%
If I ever meet a
Bella Coola, we'll

00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:01.310 align:middle line:90%
have a very simple conversation.

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:03.680 align:middle line:90%
It means seal fat.

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:07.670 align:middle line:84%
And so here's a
place where you hope

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:10.220 align:middle line:84%
there are other cues besides
effects on vowels because there

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:12.920 align:middle line:90%
are no vowels in this word.

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:16.220 align:middle line:84%
Or the Berber languages
of Northern Africa

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:18.330 align:middle line:84%
are famous for having
sounds like this.

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:21.860 align:middle line:84%
Here's the only word
I know, the only word

00:42:21.860 --> 00:42:25.400 align:middle line:84%
I know in [INAUDIBLE] Berber,
which is spoken in Morocco.

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:29.300 align:middle line:84%
It means "you
(feminine) sprained it."

00:42:29.300 --> 00:42:31.305 align:middle line:84%
And it's something
like [CLICKING]

00:42:31.305 --> 00:42:32.930 align:middle line:84%
Phonologists just
get really interested

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:34.620 align:middle line:84%
in these languages
for lots of reasons.

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:36.110 align:middle line:84%
One is the kind of thing
Joseph is asking about,

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:38.690 align:middle line:84%
how do these people figure out
what sounds they are hearing?

00:42:38.690 --> 00:42:41.930 align:middle line:84%
And the answer has to be
something like, yeah, there are

00:42:41.930 --> 00:42:43.550 align:middle line:90%
lots of good cues on vowels.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:46.010 align:middle line:84%
But there are also cues
on other kinds of things.

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:48.230 align:middle line:84%
And we have to figure
out what they are, how--

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:51.650 align:middle line:90%
what people are cueing in on.

00:42:51.650 --> 00:42:54.392 align:middle line:84%
There are other questions too,
like I was just talking about.

00:42:54.392 --> 00:42:55.850 align:middle line:84%
Syllables, which
is something we'll

00:42:55.850 --> 00:42:57.800 align:middle line:84%
talk about as we get
further into phonology.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:00.320 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, are there
any syllables in this word?

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:00.820 align:middle line:90%


00:43:00.820 --> 00:43:03.590 align:middle line:84%
To which, the answer
turns out to be yes.

00:43:03.590 --> 00:43:06.290 align:middle line:84%
So if you look carefully
at these languages,

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:10.370 align:middle line:84%
you can convince yourself that
they have syllables, yeah.

00:43:10.370 --> 00:43:12.290 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, very long answer
to Joseph's question,

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:13.957 align:middle line:84%
to which the short
version of the answer

00:43:13.957 --> 00:43:15.767 align:middle line:84%
would be the best
cues are on vowels.

00:43:15.767 --> 00:43:18.350 align:middle line:84%
But there must be cues on other
kinds of things as well, which

00:43:18.350 --> 00:43:19.865 align:middle line:90%
are not as clear.

00:43:19.865 --> 00:43:21.890 align:middle line:90%
All right?

00:43:21.890 --> 00:43:24.890 align:middle line:84%
And it's a question of
your level of tolerance

00:43:24.890 --> 00:43:27.860 align:middle line:84%
for unclarity, yeah,
or how good you are

00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:31.290 align:middle line:90%
at picking up on those cues.

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:31.790 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:43:31.790 --> 00:43:35.618 align:middle line:84%
So Praat, was i going to do
anything else with Praat?

00:43:35.618 --> 00:43:37.910 align:middle line:84%
No, those were the things I
was going to do with Praat.

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:39.670 align:middle line:90%
I will leave Praat up.

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:41.420 align:middle line:84%
I think-- actually,
we may be seeing Praat

00:43:41.420 --> 00:43:42.780 align:middle line:90%
again pretty shortly.

00:43:42.780 --> 00:43:44.960 align:middle line:84%
But I'll leave
Praat on the website

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:48.594 align:middle line:84%
so you can play around
with it at home.

00:43:48.594 --> 00:43:51.810 align:middle line:90%
Going to full screen mode.

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:52.500 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry.

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:54.330 align:middle line:90%
Go back out of screen mode.

00:43:54.330 --> 00:43:56.316 align:middle line:84%
So we've just been
talking about how

00:43:56.316 --> 00:44:01.200 align:middle line:84%
you know what sounds it
is that you are hearing.

00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:03.315 align:middle line:84%
And some of the cues
come from what's

00:44:03.315 --> 00:44:04.440 align:middle line:90%
happening during the sound.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.900 align:middle line:84%
And some of the cues come
from neighboring sounds.

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:10.440 align:middle line:84%
And the study of this is
one of the central studies

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:12.390 align:middle line:90%
of phonology and of phonetics.

00:44:12.390 --> 00:44:14.640 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you about
another, kind of, information

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:15.630 align:middle line:90%
that we have, though.

00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:18.670 align:middle line:84%
It's something called
the McGurk effect.

00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:20.170 align:middle line:84%
And I'll just
demonstrate it to you.

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:21.420 align:middle line:90%
And then, we'll talk about it.

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:25.960 align:middle line:84%
This guy is going to repeat
a syllable a couple of times.

00:44:25.960 --> 00:44:28.780 align:middle line:84%
And then, when he's done,
I'm going to ask you

00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:32.200 align:middle line:90%
what syllable you heard.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:33.370 align:middle line:90%
So here's the guy.

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:35.617 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:44:35.617 --> 00:44:36.117 align:middle line:90%
- Bah.

00:44:36.117 --> 00:44:37.108 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:37.108 --> 00:44:37.608 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:37.608 --> 00:44:38.600 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:39.100 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:40.647 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:40.647 --> 00:44:41.230 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:43.230 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm going
to turn up the volume

00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:45.292 align:middle line:84%
and do that again so
you can hear it better.

00:44:45.292 --> 00:44:47.500 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:44:47.500 --> 00:44:48.000 align:middle line:90%
- Bah.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:49.000 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:49.500 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:50.000 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:50.500 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:52.471 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:52.471 --> 00:44:52.971 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:52.971 --> 00:44:54.355 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:54.355 --> 00:44:54.855 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:54.855 --> 00:44:55.768 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:55.768 --> 00:44:56.268 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:56.268 --> 00:44:57.567 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:44:57.567 --> 00:44:58.150 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:44:58.150 --> 00:45:03.190 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: How many
of you heard a "bah"?

00:45:03.190 --> 00:45:06.610 align:middle line:90%
How many of you heard a "da"?

00:45:06.610 --> 00:45:09.695 align:middle line:90%
How many of you heard a "gah"?

00:45:09.695 --> 00:45:11.820 align:middle line:84%
Several of you are raising
your hand several times.

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:12.320 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:13.820 align:middle line:84%
You're just not
sure what you heard?

00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:15.840 align:middle line:84%
Or you thought you heard
several things at once?

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:19.150 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you thought you heard
several things at once.

00:45:19.150 --> 00:45:21.330 align:middle line:84%
So there's something
very confusing going on.

00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:22.840 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to play it again.

00:45:22.840 --> 00:45:24.420 align:middle line:84%
And I want you to
close your eyes.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:26.242 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:45:26.242 --> 00:45:28.242 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Everybody
got your eyes closed?

00:45:28.242 --> 00:45:31.116 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:45:31.116 --> 00:45:31.616 align:middle line:90%
- Bah.

00:45:31.616 --> 00:45:32.544 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:45:32.544 --> 00:45:33.044 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:45:33.044 --> 00:45:33.972 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:45:33.972 --> 00:45:34.472 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:45:34.472 --> 00:45:35.682 align:middle line:90%
Bah.

00:45:35.682 --> 00:45:37.173 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:45:37.173 --> 00:45:39.090 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'll
put the link so that you

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:40.590 align:middle line:90%
can play with this at home.

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.070 align:middle line:90%
But here's what's happening.

00:45:42.070 --> 00:45:44.340 align:middle line:84%
You're hearing a
recording of "bah."

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:45.360 align:middle line:90%
That's what he's doing.

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:46.680 align:middle line:90%
He's saying "bah."

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:49.380 align:middle line:84%
But the "bah" is being
carefully timed together

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:51.065 align:middle line:84%
with a video of
him saying "gah".

00:45:51.065 --> 00:45:52.560 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:55.410 align:middle line:84%
And so you can only
tell that it's "bah"

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:56.655 align:middle line:90%
if you don't look at him.

00:45:56.655 --> 00:45:57.720 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:57.720 --> 00:46:01.140 align:middle line:84%
So if your eyes are closed,
you hear it as "bah," yeah?

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:03.870 align:middle line:84%
But if your eyes are not
closed, if you look at him--

00:46:03.870 --> 00:46:05.760 align:middle line:84%
you should try this at
home maybe if you're

00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:07.020 align:middle line:90%
on a better quality screen.

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:09.420 align:middle line:84%
The illusion becomes
fairly clear.

00:46:09.420 --> 00:46:11.070 align:middle line:84%
People generally
report that they're

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:15.150 align:middle line:84%
hearing "dah," which is
neither what they are seeing

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:18.100 align:middle line:90%
or what they are hearing, yeah?

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:20.400 align:middle line:84%
So what this is teaching
us is that although there's

00:46:20.400 --> 00:46:22.500 align:middle line:84%
all this complicated
phonology going on,

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:25.140 align:middle line:84%
all of these things that you do
to try to figure out the speech

00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:28.470 align:middle line:84%
signal, you are also paying
attention to what you see.

00:46:28.470 --> 00:46:30.010 align:middle line:84%
So apparently,
what's going on here,

00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:32.250 align:middle line:84%
again, the recording
is of "bah."

00:46:32.250 --> 00:46:33.900 align:middle line:90%
But you're looking at this guy.

00:46:33.900 --> 00:46:37.200 align:middle line:84%
And he's clearly not saying
"bah" because his lips are not

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:38.010 align:middle line:90%
closing.

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:41.070 align:middle line:84%
And apparently, the
evidence of your eyes

00:46:41.070 --> 00:46:44.883 align:middle line:84%
is enough to convince your
brain to ignore your ears when

00:46:44.883 --> 00:46:47.050 align:middle line:84%
it's trying to figure out
what you're hearing, yeah.

00:46:47.050 --> 00:46:50.310 align:middle line:84%
So the cause of the
illusion is that because you

00:46:50.310 --> 00:46:52.830 align:middle line:84%
can see that he's not
making a bilabial closure,

00:46:52.830 --> 00:46:55.500 align:middle line:84%
your brain is rejecting
the hypothesis that it's

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:58.560 align:middle line:84%
a bilabial closure and trying to
come up with the closest sound

00:46:58.560 --> 00:46:59.310 align:middle line:90%
that it can.

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:00.930 align:middle line:90%
And you end up with confusion.

00:47:00.930 --> 00:47:01.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:03.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So then, what
would language acquisition

00:47:03.240 --> 00:47:05.100 align:middle line:84%
look like in individuals
who are born blind?

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:07.810 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
that's a really good question.

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:10.910 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether
there's any work on that,

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:14.310 align:middle line:84%
that question of whether the
McGurk effect has anything

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:15.060 align:middle line:90%
to say about that.

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:18.013 align:middle line:84%
We obviously can get along just
fine without seeing the people

00:47:18.013 --> 00:47:18.930 align:middle line:90%
that we're talking to.

00:47:18.930 --> 00:47:21.960 align:middle line:84%
We can talk to people on the
phone and all kinds of things.

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:25.080 align:middle line:84%
But we are apparently geared
to pay so much attention

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:28.020 align:middle line:84%
to visual input that
it actually overrides

00:47:28.020 --> 00:47:29.640 align:middle line:90%
the evidence of our ears.

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:31.110 align:middle line:90%
And it's a very powerful effect.

00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:32.440 align:middle line:90%
I know what's going on.

00:47:32.440 --> 00:47:33.630 align:middle line:90%
And it happens to me.

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:34.890 align:middle line:90%
You know what's going on now.

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:37.233 align:middle line:84%
Then it will happen to
if you watch this again.

00:47:37.233 --> 00:47:38.400 align:middle line:90%
Again, I'll put the link up.

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:42.010 align:middle line:84%
And you can amaze
your friends with it.

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:43.410 align:middle line:90%
It's a very robust phenomenon.

00:47:43.410 --> 00:47:45.420 align:middle line:84%
It's been demonstrated for
a bunch of different sounds

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:47.003 align:middle line:84%
and in a bunch of
different languages.

00:47:47.003 --> 00:47:49.110 align:middle line:90%
It's not just an English thing.

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:52.260 align:middle line:90%
It's all over the place.

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:55.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, am I done
with other things?

00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:58.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, now I am about to
start talking about something

00:47:58.740 --> 00:48:00.300 align:middle line:90%
which is pretty complicated.

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:04.470 align:middle line:90%
And so I'll just give you--

00:48:04.470 --> 00:48:07.030 align:middle line:84%
yeah, so I'll tell
you briefly what I'm

00:48:07.030 --> 00:48:08.280 align:middle line:90%
going to talk about next time.

00:48:08.280 --> 00:48:09.720 align:middle line:84%
And we'll just start
with this next time

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:11.470 align:middle line:84%
because I don't think
we have time to talk

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:14.162 align:middle line:90%
about this in any detail.

00:48:14.162 --> 00:48:16.620 align:middle line:84%
We've been talking-- well,
here, let me show you something.

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:20.700 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll talk
more about it next time.

00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:21.395 align:middle line:90%
Back to Praat.

00:48:21.395 --> 00:48:25.730 align:middle line:90%


00:48:25.730 --> 00:48:26.390 align:middle line:90%
"Pie.

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:27.170 align:middle line:90%
Bye.

00:48:27.170 --> 00:48:29.366 align:middle line:90%
Spy."

00:48:29.366 --> 00:48:30.290 align:middle line:90%
Ah, darn.

00:48:30.290 --> 00:48:31.683 align:middle line:90%
I failed to save that.

00:48:31.683 --> 00:48:32.600 align:middle line:90%
Hang on just a second.

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:35.350 align:middle line:90%


00:48:35.350 --> 00:48:36.010 align:middle line:90%
Pi.

00:48:36.010 --> 00:48:36.695 align:middle line:90%
Bye.

00:48:36.695 --> 00:48:37.195 align:middle line:90%
Spy.

00:48:37.195 --> 00:48:42.110 align:middle line:90%


00:48:42.110 --> 00:48:46.775 align:middle line:84%
So that's me saying
"pie, bye, spy."

00:48:46.775 --> 00:48:47.506 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:48:47.506 --> 00:48:48.877 align:middle line:90%


00:48:48.877 --> 00:48:49.460 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:52.220 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
I'm sorry, the--

00:48:52.220 --> 00:48:53.540 align:middle line:90%
everything is up again.

00:48:53.540 --> 00:48:57.992 align:middle line:84%
So here's-- let's
just listen to "pie."

00:48:57.992 --> 00:48:59.450 align:middle line:84%
Actually, let's
just look at "pie."

00:48:59.450 --> 00:49:01.990 align:middle line:90%


00:49:01.990 --> 00:49:02.770 align:middle line:90%
Can I do that?

00:49:02.770 --> 00:49:05.428 align:middle line:90%
Come on, you.

00:49:05.428 --> 00:49:06.289 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:49:06.289 --> 00:49:07.310 align:middle line:90%


00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:07.970 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:10.553 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So that's the
spectrogram of me saying "pie."

00:49:10.553 --> 00:49:13.730 align:middle line:84%
And you can see-- here,
I'll use my cursor instead.

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:16.670 align:middle line:84%
What's going on here,
my-- here's the stop.

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:20.930 align:middle line:84%
And then, this little
burst is my lips opening.

00:49:20.930 --> 00:49:24.020 align:middle line:84%
And then, there's a little
burst of white noise.

00:49:24.020 --> 00:49:25.960 align:middle line:84%
And then, we start
in on the vowel.

00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:30.440 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the formants,
the black bars that indicate

00:49:30.440 --> 00:49:31.970 align:middle line:90%
that we're doing a vowel.

00:49:31.970 --> 00:49:34.430 align:middle line:84%
This little burst of
white noise is the period

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:39.980 align:middle line:84%
that elapses in between my lips
opening and my vocal chords

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:41.690 align:middle line:90%
beginning to engage.

00:49:41.690 --> 00:49:43.910 align:middle line:84%
So there's this period
where there's nothing

00:49:43.910 --> 00:49:45.940 align:middle line:90%
coming out of my lungs but air.

00:49:45.940 --> 00:49:47.690 align:middle line:84%
There's always air
coming out of my lungs.

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:48.607 align:middle line:90%
Let me try that again.

00:49:48.607 --> 00:49:50.900 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing coming out
of my vocal tract but air.

00:49:50.900 --> 00:49:51.830 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:49:51.830 --> 00:49:53.720 align:middle line:84%
My vocal chords
aren't vibrating yet.

00:49:53.720 --> 00:49:54.950 align:middle line:90%
And it's not long.

00:49:54.950 --> 00:49:57.320 align:middle line:84%
It lasts some
fraction of a second.

00:49:57.320 --> 00:49:58.820 align:middle line:90%
How long does it last?

00:49:58.820 --> 00:50:02.960 align:middle line:84%
It lasts-- who cares
how long it lasts?

00:50:02.960 --> 00:50:03.643 align:middle line:90%
Do I care?

00:50:03.643 --> 00:50:05.060 align:middle line:84%
Do I care long
enough to find out?

00:50:05.060 --> 00:50:06.620 align:middle line:90%
It lasts less time than that.

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:08.780 align:middle line:90%
It lasts 0.06 seconds.

00:50:08.780 --> 00:50:11.040 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, there we go.

00:50:11.040 --> 00:50:13.680 align:middle line:84%
So that's what's
going on in "pie."

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:15.530 align:middle line:90%
Now let's look at "spy."

00:50:15.530 --> 00:50:18.450 align:middle line:90%


00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:19.276 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:50:19.276 --> 00:50:20.285 align:middle line:90%


00:50:20.285 --> 00:50:21.120 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:23.120 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And you
can see-- and actually,

00:50:23.120 --> 00:50:24.560 align:middle line:90%
allow me to let you hear.

00:50:24.560 --> 00:50:25.913 align:middle line:90%
So here's the "s."

00:50:25.913 --> 00:50:27.330 align:middle line:84%
This is what
fricatives look like,

00:50:27.330 --> 00:50:29.000 align:middle line:90%
these bursts of light and noise.

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:32.180 align:middle line:84%
Lots more energy up
here for the "s."

00:50:32.180 --> 00:50:33.175 align:middle line:90%
Here's the stop.

00:50:33.175 --> 00:50:34.550 align:middle line:84%
You can see what
stops look like.

00:50:34.550 --> 00:50:35.450 align:middle line:90%
It's blank.

00:50:35.450 --> 00:50:37.718 align:middle line:84%
Here are my lips opening
for the end of the stop.

00:50:37.718 --> 00:50:39.635 align:middle line:84%
And let's just play it
starting with the stop.

00:50:39.635 --> 00:50:40.302 align:middle line:90%
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:50:40.302 --> 00:50:42.848 align:middle line:90%


00:50:42.848 --> 00:50:44.480 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:47.190 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: How many
of you hear that as "pie"?

00:50:47.190 --> 00:50:48.840 align:middle line:84%
How many of you
here it as "bye"?

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:53.350 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so this is a
non-aspirated voiceless stop.

00:50:53.350 --> 00:50:54.780 align:middle line:90%
So my lips are opening.

00:50:54.780 --> 00:50:56.760 align:middle line:84%
And then, my vocal cords
get busy right away.

00:50:56.760 --> 00:51:00.000 align:middle line:84%
You can see there isn't a big
gap right in between the "p'

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:02.310 align:middle line:90%
and what's happening after it.

00:51:02.310 --> 00:51:04.230 align:middle line:84%
So we will talk more
about this next time.

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:06.630 align:middle line:84%
But this is what's
called aspiration.

00:51:06.630 --> 00:51:09.422 align:middle line:84%
English has aspiration in some
places, but not in others.

00:51:09.422 --> 00:51:11.380 align:middle line:84%
So we'll do this
demonstration again next time.

00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:13.750 align:middle line:84%
And then, we'll talk
about what it means.

00:51:13.750 --> 00:51:15.660 align:middle line:84%
So for now, I think
we're done unless anybody

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:18.360 align:middle line:84%
has any questions
about any of this.

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:19.350 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:51:19.350 --> 00:51:21.900 align:middle line:84%
See you not on Tuesday,
but on Thursday.

00:51:21.900 --> 00:51:24.320 align:middle line:84%
So we don't have
class on Tuesday.

00:51:24.320 --> 00:51:32.000 align:middle line:90%